The  Apple Desktop Bus is the single‐master, multiple‐slave, low‐
speed serial bus interface used by Macintosh computers to connect
input devices such as keyboards, mice, trackpads, trackballs, and
graphics tablets to the machine.  provides support for the  Apple
Desktop  Bus  as found on all supported mac68k models, as well as
macppc models with on‐board (PowerBooks and models).  The  driver
accesses  the controller using the so‐called method.  This method
of access bypasses the Macintosh ROM and uses only  routines  for
ADB  access.   This is the only method supported on macppc and is
the default for mac68k systems.  On mac68k systems  there  is  an
alternate method of accessing the controller.  With the Macintosh
ROM Glue method, the routines written for MacOS are used.  To en‐
able  this  method  of access, uncomment the line: in your kernel
configuration file.  The call is used to control  the  event  de‐
vice.   The following is a list of available commands: Get Device
